<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="137" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>C5 — revocation that actually revokes: inactive contact and inactive root confer nothing, grant-cache invalidation reaches the CIAM entry and every member of an organization, and the reconciliation job's enable/write posture is decided and recorded</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-30, session 27; contact-plane group, defect C5)</phase>
    <gate>after-136-109-141</gate>
    <status>in-progress</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Authorization read path and cache invalidation on every grant-write endpoint (.cs); tags bff-api/auth/security; removes access that deactivated contacts and records keep today.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Security-sensitive, multi-surface brownfield change with opposite fail directions (read guards must fail closed; invalidation failures must degrade to the TTL, never to an error or a grant) and an identity-binding trap on the CIAM contact lookup.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Hot-file rule: edits Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalParticipationService.cs, AccessibleRecordSetService.cs and ExternalCallerContext.cs (tasks 109, 131, 132, 135, 136, 138 and 141 edit the same files — 138 also extends RootRecordFlags and the flag-read $select, and 132 changes the grant-set fault path; serialize with both, whichever lands second rebases) and four Api/ExternalAccess grant-write endpoints (task 138/139 edit the grant core in GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs); may touch Infrastructure/DI/ExternalAccessModule.cs (task 134).</parallel-reason>
    <deps>136, 109, 141</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, dataverse, cache, background, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6-8 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 26 verified defect C5 [MEDIUM] (notes/session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md § C5; UC2 "REVOCATION/FAIL-SOFT", UC3 "REVOCATION"). Partially tracked by #1006 (ISS-026) and #999 (ISS-020); the untracked parts — contact deactivation, root deactivation, the CIAM-tid invalidation miss, organization-grant invalidation, and the reconciliation job's posture — are owned here.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Firm X is disengaged: operations deactivates the organization, the contact and the matter. The contact keeps reading the matter's documents until each grant's expiry date. Deactivating a contact or a root record revokes nothing, a revoke clears the grant cache under the wrong tenant id so a CIAM caller keeps cached access for the TTL, and an organization-grant revoke clears nothing at all. Close those holes at read time, make invalidation reach every cached entry it should, confirm the already-tracked parts are closed by the tasks that own them, and get an owner decision on the reconciliation job.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE BFF security engineer — the external grant read path (ExternalParticipationService), the evaluator's root-flag read, the grant-write endpoints and the ITenantCache contract. Revocation is the product here: a read guard that fails open, or an invalidation that silently misses, is the defect coming back.</role>

  <goal>(1) An INACTIVE contact confers nothing through any contact-sourced term on either plane, checked LIVE on every composition (a contact deactivated after sign-in, behind a warm 10-minute identity cache, loses access on the next request), and a systemuser's inactive linked contact contributes no contact-sourced access. The SIGN-IN half — an oid bound to an inactive contact denies with its own code and never falls through to the email bind — is implemented by task 141 (C7) on both planes and VERIFIED here. (2) An INACTIVE project, matter or work assignment confers nothing contact-sourced on either plane (the systemuser's own membership term is unaffected). (3) Every grant-write path invalidates every cache entry that can hold the affected contacts' grant sets — including the CIAM-tenant entry and every active member of an affected organization. (4) The tracked parts (#1006, #999, start date D-10) are verified closed by task 109 on BOTH planes, with the evidence recorded. (5) The reconciliation job's enable and write posture is decided by the owner and recorded, and the configuration matches the decision.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 LIVE, verified 2026-09-30 against source.

WHAT THE GRANT READ CHECKS TODAY. Contact grants: grant `statecode eq 0` plus the expiry predicate only (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalParticipationService.cs:61-62`); organization grants: the same (`:68-72`); the membership junction: `statecode eq 0` only (`:1101-1103`). Nothing checks the contact's `statecode`, `sprk_organization.statecode`, the junction's `sprk_enddate` / `sprk_startdate`, or the root record's `statecode`. There are no plugins (D-1); `/close-project` is an explicit project-only cascade.

CONTACT RESOLUTION HAS NO STATE FILTER. CIAM: `ResolveContactByOidAsync` (`:366-398`, query at `:375`) and the email fallback `ResolveContactRowByEmailAsync` (`:414-453`, query at `:423`, `$top=1`). ⚠️ TRAP: simply adding `statecode eq 0` to the oid lookup makes an inactive-but-bound contact look "unbound", so `ResolveExternalContactAsync` (`:314-361`) falls through to the email path and can BIND the caller's oid to a different active contact that shares the email. An oid match on an inactive contact must end in a denial, never in the email fallback. Workforce: contact resolution lives in `Services/Ai/Membership/IdentityNormalizationService.cs` (oid path `:533-576`, email path `:583-605`) behind a 10-minute identity cache (`:55`), so a contact-state check that lives only there is stale for up to 10 minutes; the check must be LIVE per composition. OWNERSHIP: task 141 (C7) rewrites contact resolution on both planes and already carries the inactive-contact sign-in rule (141 constraint "Scope: inactive contacts", and its criterion that `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy` returns a distinct inactive deny code). This task depends on 141 and does not re-implement that rule; it owns the LIVE per-composition guard, which resolution alone cannot provide.

THE ROOT-FLAG READ IS ALREADY LIVE AND BATCHED. `GetRootRecordFlagsAsync` (`:541-624`) selects `sprk_issecure,sprk_accesspermission` per root type (`:572-574`) with no cache, and fails closed to `RootRecordFlags.Unreadable` (`ExternalCallerContext.cs:139-152`). Adding `statecode` to that `$select` costs no extra round trip. `ApplyVetoPipeline` (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:357-407`) already has the "remove contact-sourced contributions, keep the systemuser's own term" shape (the Restricted slot, `:391-406`). `ExternalDataService.GetProjectsAsync` / `GetProjectByIdAsync` select `statecode` but never filter on it (`ExternalDataService.cs:181`, `:197`).

THE INVALIDATION MISS. The grant cache key is `tenant:{tid}:external-access-grant:{contactId}:v{n}` (`TenantCache.cs:257-258`), where `tid` is the REQUEST's tenant (`ExternalParticipationService.cs:193-202`, `:290-296`). A CIAM caller's entry is written under the CIAM tenant; a workforce caller's under the workforce tenant. Every grant-write endpoint runs on the workforce admin group and removes only `tenant:{admin tid}` — so the CIAM entry survives for the 60-second TTL (`:18`): `RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:194-225` (removal at `:208-210`, tenant from `:315-317`), `GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:491-530` (`:513-514`), `ProjectClosureEndpoint.cs:484` (tenant from `:505-507`), `SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs:339-353`. `ExternalParticipationService.InvalidateAsync` (`:252-284`) exists but the endpoints each carry their own copy. Organization grants: the revoke (`RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:198-205`) and the grant (`GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:501-510`) invalidate NOTHING; `SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs:304-337` already fans out to members through `ExternalOrganizationMembership.QueryActiveMembersAsync` (`RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:747-800`; bound `MaxMembersPerSweep = 200` at `:765`), but skips an organization over the bound (`:320-327`). (`notes/investigation/04-grant-lifecycle.md:116` wrongly claims 0-second revoke latency — correct it.)

WHAT IS ALREADY TRACKED, AND WHO OWNS IT (verified from the task files):
- Organization deactivation, READ guard (#1006 / ISS-026 read half): task 109 (pending) — its acceptance includes "an active grant and an active membership whose parent sprk_organization is INACTIVE confer nothing".
- Membership end date (#999 / ISS-020) and start date (owner D-10): task 109 (pending), verified and closed by task 110.
- Deactivation WRITER for inactive-organization grants, ended memberships and undated grants (#1006 write half, ISS-020 part 4, D-1 option B): task 117 (completed) — `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob`, registered `enabled: false` (`Infrastructure/DI/ExternalAccessModule.cs:360-372`) with writes gated on `ExternalAccess:Reconciliation:WritesEnabled`, default report-only (`Services/ExternalAccess/ExternalAccessReconciliationJob.cs:109`, `:208-209`).
Because `GetGrantSetAsync` is shared by both planes, task 109's guards reach CIAM too — but only once task 135 routes CIAM through the same code; this task VERIFIES that end to end.

THE JOB'S POSTURE IS UNTRACKED. "Enabling it is an owner action" (task 117; `ExternalAccessModule.cs:360-364`), and no issue or task tracks that decision. Owner D-2 part 3 requires the affected rows to be counted and listed before anything that removes access ships.</background>
